# How to cite this game, for anybody who writes about it — a talk, a paper on
# making software for children, a list of Godot projects. GitHub reads this file
# and offers the citation on the repository page.
cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Aava"
message: "If you write about this software, please cite it as below."
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Mukharbek
    family-names: Organokov
    email: mukharbek.organokov@gmail.com
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-3093-3456"
abstract: >-
  An open world that comes alive as you build in it: a 3D exploration and
  building game for children, written in GDScript on Godot 4 and built around a
  procedural valley generated from a seed. Everything a child builds makes the
  valley more alive, and the more alive it is, the more it gives back.
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.22965441
    description: >-
      The concept DOI, which always resolves to the newest release. Cite a
      version DOI instead when it matters which build somebody actually played.
keywords:
  - game
  - godot
  - gdscript
  - children
  - procedural-generation
  - android
license: AGPL-3.0-or-later
repository-code: "https://github.com/kabartay/aava"
version: 0.4.1
date-released: "2026-09-21"
